    <h3>Data Completeness</h3>
    <div class="note">
        <strong>No missing values detected</strong> across any of the six columns (id, node_id, fuel_type, price_pence, recorded_at, source_updated_at). All {total_records:,} records are complete and parseable.
    </div>
    <p>Price values are integers or single-decimal pence per litre. Typical UK fuel prices in this period range from approximately 120p to 210p per litre depending on fuel type. All timestamps are in UTC (ISO 8601 format).</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 2: Price Distribution ═══ -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>2. Price Distribution by Fuel Type</h2>
    <p>The distribution of prices across fuel types is the centrepiece of this analysis. The table and charts below show mean, median, spread, and quartile statistics for each fuel type.</p>

    <div class="kpi-row">
        {kpi_card('E10 Mean', f'{price_stats.loc["E10","mean"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["E10","mean"]), color=get_fuel_color("E10"))}
        {kpi_card('B7 Std Mean', f'{price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","mean"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","mean"]), color=get_fuel_color("B7_STANDARD"))}
        {kpi_card('E5 Mean', f'{price_stats.loc["E5","mean"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["E5","mean"]), color=get_fuel_color("E5"))}
        {kpi_card('B7 Prem Mean', f'{price_stats.loc["B7_PREMIUM","mean"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["B7_PREMIUM","mean"]), color=get_fuel_color("B7_PREMIUM"))}
    </div>

    <h3>Descriptive Statistics</h3>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Records</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>25th %ile</th><th>75th %ile</th></tr>
        {price_table_rows}
    </table>

    <h3>Box Plot — All Fuel Types</h3>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{boxplot_b64}" alt="Box plot of price distribution by fuel type"></div>

    <h3>Price Density — Top 3 Fuel Types (Violin Plot)</h3>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{violin_b64}" alt="Violin plot of top 3 fuel types"></div>

    <h3>Price Histograms — Top 3 Fuel Types</h3>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{hist_b64}" alt="Histograms of top 3 fuel types"></div>

    <p><strong>{most_common}</strong> dominates the dataset with {fuel_type_counts[most_common]:,} records ({fuel_type_counts[most_common]/total_records*100:.1f}% of all observations). <strong>{cheapest_ft}</strong> is the cheapest fuel type on average ({price_stats.loc[cheapest_ft,'mean']:.1f}p/l), while <strong>{most_expensive_ft}</strong> is the most expensive ({price_stats.loc[most_expensive_ft,'mean']:.1f}p/l).</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 3: Price Spread ═══ -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>3. Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</h2>
    <p>Price spread is a direct indicator of market competitiveness. A wide range suggests consumers can save meaningfully by shopping around; a narrow IQR suggests most stations cluster around a common price point.</p>

    <div class="kpi-row">
        {kpi_card('E10 Range', f'{price_stats.loc["E10","range"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["E10","range"]), color=get_fuel_color("E10"))}
        {kpi_card('E10 IQR', f'{price_stats.loc["E10","iqr"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["E10","iqr"]), color=get_fuel_color("E10"))}
        {kpi_card('B7 Std Range', f'{price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","range"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","range"]), color=get_fuel_color("B7_STANDARD"))}
        {kpi_card('B7 Std IQR', f'{price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","iqr"]:.1f}p', fmt_pence(price_stats.loc["B7_STANDARD","iqr"]), color=get_fuel_color("B7_STANDARD"))}
    </div>

    <h3>Spread Summary</h3>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Max−Min (p/l)</th><th>Max−Min (£/l)</th><th>IQR (p/l)</th><th>IQR (£/l)</th><th>Typical Band (25th–75th)</th></tr>
        {spread_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{spread_b64}" alt="Price spread and IQR by fuel type"></div>

    <div class="alert">
        <strong>Policy significance:</strong> For <strong>{largest_spread_ft}</strong>, the max−min spread is {price_stats.loc[largest_spread_ft,'range']:.1f}p/l ({fmt_pence(price_stats.loc[largest_spread_ft,'range'])}). This means a consumer filling a 55-litre tank could pay up to <strong>£{price_stats.loc[largest_spread_ft,'range']*55/100:.2f}</strong> more depending on which forecourt they choose — a material difference for household budgets.
    </div>

    <p>The IQR for the two most common fuels — E10 ({price_stats.loc['E10','iqr']:.1f}p) and B7_STANDARD ({price_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD','iqr']:.1f}p) — indicates that the middle 50% of prices are contained within a relatively narrow band. However, the long tails visible in the box plots show that a meaningful number of stations charge substantially above or below the median, creating real savings opportunities for informed consumers.</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 4: Update Freshness ═══ -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>
    <p>The lag between <code>source_updated_at</code> (when a retailer last updated their price) and <code>recorded_at</code> (when the data was collected) indicates how fresh the reported prices are. Long lags undermine the utility of the open data scheme for consumers.</p>

    <div class="kpi-row">
        {kpi_card('Median Lag', f'{overall_median_lag:.1f}h', 'Across all records', color=COLORS['secondary'])}
        {kpi_card('Mean Lag', f'{overall_mean_lag:.1f}h', 'Skewed by outliers', color=COLORS['warning'])}
        {kpi_card('Lag &gt; 24h', f'{pct_over_24:.1f}%', 'Records with stale prices', color=COLORS['accent'])}
        {kpi_card('Lag &gt; 72h', f'{pct_over_72:.1f}%', 'Critically stale', color=COLORS['accent'])}
    </div>

    <h3>Update Lag by Fuel Type</h3>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Median Lag</th><th>Mean Lag</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>% Over 24h</th><th>% Over 72h</th></tr>
        {lag_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{lag_hist_b64}" alt="Update lag distribution histogram"></div>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{lag_box_b64}" alt="Update lag box plot by fuel type"></div>

    <h3>Stale Data Flag</h3>
    <div class="alert">
        <strong>{stale_stations:,} stations ({pct_stale:.1f}%)</strong> have at least one record with an update lag exceeding 72 hours (3 days). These stations may not be promptly updating their reported prices, potentially misleading consumers who rely on the Fuel Finder service.
    </div>

    <p>The bimodal pattern visible in the lag histogram suggests two populations of retailers: those updating frequently (sub-hourly or daily) and those updating infrequently (weekly or less). This disparity affects the real-time reliability of the open data scheme and warrants further regulatory attention.</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 5: Station Coverage ═══ -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>5. Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</h2>
    <p>Not all stations report prices for every fuel type. Coverage breadth affects how representative the data is for each fuel type and determines whether consumers can rely on the service for less common fuels.</p>

    <h3>Fuel Type Availability Across Stations</h3>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Stations Reporting</th><th>% of All Stations</th></tr>
        {coverage_table_rows}
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{coverage_b64}" alt="Station coverage by fuel type"></div>

    <h3>How Many Fuel Types Does Each Station Report?</h3>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{fuel_per_station_b64}" alt="Distribution of fuel types per station"></div>

    <div class="note">
        <strong>E10 and B7_STANDARD</strong> are reported by the vast majority of stations, reflecting their status as the standard unleaded and diesel grades. Premium fuels (E5, B7_PREMIUM) and specialty fuels (B10, SDV) have significantly lower coverage, which may limit price comparison utility for consumers seeking those products.
    </div>
</div>

