    <h2>6. Daily Price Trend</h2>
    <p>The daily average price for the three most common fuel types over the data period shows how prices evolved.</p>
    <div class="chart-container"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{trend_b64}" alt="Daily average price trend"></div>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SECTION 7: Key Findings ═══ -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>7. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

    <ol class="findings-list">
        <li><strong>Significant price disparities exist for common fuels.</strong> The price range for {largest_spread_ft} spans {price_stats.loc[largest_spread_ft,'range']:.1f}p/l, meaning a consumer filling a 55L tank could overpay by up to £{price_stats.loc[largest_spread_ft,'range']*55/100:.2f}. <em>Policy implication:</em> Consumer education and price comparison tools remain essential for market transparency.</li>

        <li><strong>The interquartile range is relatively narrow for E10 and B7_STANDARD</strong> ({price_stats.loc['E10','iqr']:.1f}p and {price_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD','iqr']:.1f}p respectively), indicating that most stations price within a tight band. However, outliers on both ends create real savings or cost penalties for consumers at the margins. <em>Policy implication:</em> Investigate whether outlier-high prices reflect geographic isolation (captive markets) or other structural factors.</li>

        <li><strong>Update lag is a material concern.</strong> {pct_over_24:.1f}% of records have a source-to-recorded lag exceeding 24 hours, and {pct_stale:.1f}% of stations have at least one record older than 72 hours. <em>Policy implication:</em> Consider mandating more frequent price updates, particularly for retailers showing chronically stale data, to improve the reliability of the Fuel Finder service.</li>

        <li><strong>Premium fuel coverage is limited.</strong> While E10 and B7_STANDARD are reported by most stations, premium fuels like B7_PREMIUM and E5 are available at far fewer locations. <em>Policy implication:</em> Expanding mandatory reporting to cover all sold fuel types at every station would improve consumer choice transparency.</li>

        <li><strong>Specialty fuels (B10, SDV) have very sparse data</strong> with few reporting stations and wide price variance, making it difficult for consumers to compare. <em>Policy implication:</em> As alternative fuels grow in market share, ensuring robust reporting coverage will be important for emerging fuel markets.</li>

        <li><strong>The dataset covers {unique_stations:,} unique forecourts</strong> over a {(date_range_end - date_range_start).days}-day period with {total_records:,} total records, providing a statistically robust foundation for pricing analysis. No data quality issues (nulls, malformed timestamps) were detected.</li>
    </ol>
